<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5392"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 8:34 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This is the postcard that gave the location of drop #8, the cereal box. <BR> <BR>The stamp on the postcard appears to me to be a swipe at Charlie Otero and the other surviving Otero siblings.  There are three of them altogether, and we know that they lived with relatives in New Mexico after the murders.  BTK, being a jerk, picked a symbolic stamp.  To mark the anniverary of the Otero crime, but also a way of trying to antagonize the surviving Oteros from a safe distance.  <BR> <BR>The stamp&#39;s from New Mexico and has three figures on it, they look like three children, or one older figure with two children -- pretty close reference to Charlie and his two surviving younger siblings. <BR> <BR>Where would BTK have gotten this stamp and when?  Is the only way to get a N.M. stamp to travel to N.M. and get it at the post office there?  &#40;I&#39;m in Canada, I don&#39;t know the workings of the US Post Office in that much detail, sorry.&#41;  Do we know the location of the postmark for the postcard?  Was it mailed in Kansas, N.M. or elsewhere?   <BR> <BR>If BTK had to go to N.M. to get the stamp, this could be a warped way of trying to communicate to Charlie Otero and the surviving Otero siblings that he was in their territory so to speak.  Also, for him to do this, he&#39;s fairly mobile, he&#39;s actively travelling in and out of state. <BR> <BR>And what is the postcard on the front page of this site about??  Who is I.P. Frehley? <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5392-->
<!--Post: 5400--><!--Time: 1107106998-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5400"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiquitah<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiquitah<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 9:43 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I believe it is Tom&#39;s joke. <BR>I PEE FREELY.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5400-->
<!--Post: 5435--><!--Time: 1107119106-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5435"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Audreyamw<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Audreyamw<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, January 30, 2005 - 1:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->OK, I found some info on the stamp, which is part of a pre-stamped postal card.  It was sold throughout the USA beginning in 2002: <BR> <BR>&#34;Philatelic News <BR>FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE <BR>May 17, 2002 <BR>Stamp Release #02-032 <BR> <BR>Postal Service To Issue Stamped Postal Card Honoring Carlsbad Caverns National Park <BR> <BR>WASHINGTON - The U.S. Postal Service will issue a 23-cent stamped card honoring Carlsbad Caverns National Park. The park is located in the southeastern corner of New Mexico. <BR> <BR>The new stamped postal card will be dedicated at the Carlsbad Caverns National Park Visitors Center in Carlsbad, N.M., on Friday, June 7, at 10 a.m. at a first day of issue ceremony. The ceremony is free and open to the public. The 23-cent stamped postal card will officially go on sale nationwide at all post offices on June 7.&#34; <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5435-->
